# Literature-Guided CSF Protein Candidates for ALS Progression-Rate Classification

## How this list was built

This shortlist is designed to be **dataset-independent** and therefore useful before any feature selection on your own mass-spectrometry data. I prioritized proteins that satisfy three conditions in descending order of weight: **direct human ALS CSF evidence tied to progression rate, survival, or ALSFRS-R decline; human CSF detectability; and a clear mechanistic link to a recognized ALS process** such as glial neuroinflammation, complement/coagulation activation, axonal degeneration, extracellular matrix remodeling, or proteostasis failure. Where the ALS literature was thin, I retained proteins only if there was a strong mechanistic rationale plus **human CSF detectability in another neurodegenerative or neuroinflammatory disease**. citeturn34view0turn31search1turn27search3turn27search14

A practical pattern emerges from the literature you would likely want your panel to reflect. The **strongest human ALS CSF progression signals** are concentrated in **chitinase-related glial activation**, **neurofilament-based axonal injury**, and a smaller set of **proteostasis/lysosomal markers** such as **GPNMB, UCHL1, APP fragments, progranulin, and cystatin C**. Coagulation/complement and ECM markers are biologically coherent, but in most cases they remain **less directly validated for fast-vs-slow ALS progression in human CSF**. citeturn28search7turn36search1turn34view0turn31search5turn38search1turn37search4turn26search5turn39search2

## Glial neuroinflammation and innate immune activation

**CHIT1 — chitotriosidase-1.** CHIT1 is one of the most reproducible CSF inflammatory proteins in ALS and is mechanistically attractive because it reflects activated microglia/macrophages within the spinal cord white matter rather than generic systemic inflammation. In the multicenter early-ALS study, CSF CHIT1 was explicitly evaluated for **prognostic value**, and earlier pathology-biomarker work tied elevated CSF CHIT1 to microglial/macrophage activation and suggested utility for predicting progression; broader chitinase panels also linked CHIT1 to clinical heterogeneity in ALS. **Evidence tier: Strong.** Key citations: Steinacker P et al., 2021, *Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis and Frontotemporal Degeneration*, “Chitotriosidase as biomarker for early stage amyotrophic lateral sclerosis: a multicenter study”; Thompson AG et al., 2019, *Journal of Neurology, Neurosurgery & Psychiatry*, “CSF chitinase proteins in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis”; Varghese AM et al., 2018, *Annals of Clinical and Translational Neurology*, “Chitotriosidase (CHIT1) is increased in microglia and macrophages in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis spinal cord and cerebrospinal fluid.” citeturn28search7turn36search2turn18search12

**CHI3L1 — chitinase-3-like protein 1, YKL-40.** CHI3L1 is best viewed as an astroglial/microglial inflammatory readout that rises alongside tissue-level glial activation and spinal cord pathology. In sporadic ALS, CSF YKL-40 was linked to worse outcome, and a 2024 study again found that **high CSF YKL-40, together with NfL, related to poor outcome**; chitinase-panel studies further support its association with ALS severity/progression biology. **Evidence tier: Strong.** Key citations: Andrés-Benito P et al., 2018, *Aging*, “YKL40 in sporadic amyotrophic lateral sclerosis: cerebrospinal fluid levels as a prognosis marker of disease progression”; Rosén C et al., 2024, *Journal of the Neurological Sciences*, “High levels of neurofilament light and YKL-40 in cerebrospinal fluid are related to poor outcome in ALS”; Thompson AG et al., 2019, *JNNP*, “CSF chitinase proteins in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis.” citeturn36search1turn22search0turn36search2

**CHI3L2 — chitinase-3-like protein 2.** CHI3L2 has moved from a plausible chitinase-family marker to a more compelling prognostic candidate because the 2024 CSF proteomic study found it not only discriminated fast from slow/intermediate ALS groups, but also correlated with **prospective** progression rate. That matters methodologically because many older biomarkers correlate only with retrospective ALSFRS-derived slopes; CHI3L2 is one of the few with explicit prospective support. **Evidence tier: Strong.** Key citations: Nakamura K et al., 2024, *Frontiers in Neurology*, “Neuroinflammation and glycosylation-related cerebrospinal fluid proteins for predicting functional decline in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis: a proteomic study”; Thompson AG et al., 2019, *JNNP*, “CSF chitinase proteins in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis.” citeturn34view0turn35search4turn33search7turn36search2

**GPNMB — glycoprotein non-metastatic melanoma protein B.** GPNMB is especially attractive for discovery-mass-spec work because it is an extracellular or shed glycoprotein with a coherent biology at the intersection of glial activation, lysosomal stress, and attempted neuroprotection. In the 2024 prospective proteomic study, CSF GPNMB had one of the best AUCs for discriminating fast from slow progression and correlated with prospective progression rate; an earlier targeted MRM study found higher CSF GPNMB in ALS and enrichment in **short-lived** patients. **Evidence tier: Strong.** Key citations: Nakamura K et al., 2024, *Frontiers in Neurology*, “Neuroinflammation and glycosylation-related cerebrospinal fluid proteins for predicting functional decline in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis: a proteomic study”; Zhu S et al., 2019, *Molecular Neurobiology*, “Targeted Multiple Reaction Monitoring Analysis of CSF Identifies UCHL1 and GPNMB as Candidate Biomarkers for ALS.” citeturn34view0turn33search7turn31search5

**GPC1 — glypican-1.** GPC1 broadens the inflammatory axis into glycosylation and axon-guidance biology. The 2024 ALS CSF proteomic study found that GPC1 discriminated fast versus slow groups and correlated with prospective progression rate, while the authors discuss mechanistic relevance through SLIT2-linked axon-repulsion and reactive astrocyte biology. **Evidence tier: Strong.** Key citations: Nakamura K et al., 2024, *Frontiers in Neurology*, “Neuroinflammation and glycosylation-related cerebrospinal fluid proteins for predicting functional decline in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis: a proteomic study.” citeturn34view0turn33search7

**GFAP — glial fibrillary acidic protein.** GFAP is a biofluid marker of reactive astrogliosis and therefore highly plausible in ALS, where astrocyte reactivity is a core component of progression biology. In the same 2024 ALS proteomic study, GFAP showed relatively high discrimination between slow and fast progressors, although it did **not** remain significantly correlated with prospective progression rate in multivariable analysis, so it is best treated as a supportive rather than top-tier prognostic candidate; human CSF detectability is very well established in AD and PD. **Evidence tier: Moderate.** Key citations: Nakamura K et al., 2024, *Frontiers in Neurology*, “Neuroinflammation and glycosylation-related cerebrospinal fluid proteins for predicting functional decline in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis: a proteomic study”; Benedet AL et al., 2021, *JAMA Neurology*, “Differences Between Plasma and Cerebrospinal Fluid Glial Fibrillary Acidic Protein Levels Across the Alzheimer Disease Continuum.” citeturn34view0turn22search9

**CCL2 — C-C motif chemokine ligand 2, MCP-1.** CCL2 remains mechanistically important because the CCL2–CCR2 axis recruits monocytes and amplifies toxic microglial states; experimental ALS work supports a role for CCR2-positive myeloid infiltration in worsening pathology. Human ALS CSF studies show intrathecal MCP-1 production and elevated CSF MCP-1, but direct progression-rate data are weaker than for chitinases or neurofilaments. **Evidence tier: Moderate.** Key citations: Baron P et al., 2005, *Journal of the Neurological Sciences*, “Intrathecal synthesis of monocyte chemoattractant protein-1 (MCP-1) in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis”; Cui LY et al., 2020, *Neurología*, “Increased cerebrospinal fluid levels of cytokines monocyte chemoattractant protein-1 (MCP-1) and macrophage inflammatory protein-1β (MIP-1β) in patients with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis”; Komori T et al., 2020, *Acta Neuropathologica Communications*, “CCR2 is localized in microglia and neurons, as well as infiltrating monocytes, in ALS spinal cord and accelerates pathology.” citeturn21search4turn21search7turn20search1

**TREM2 — triggering receptor expressed on myeloid cells 2, assayed in CSF as sTREM2.** TREM2 is attractive biologically because it tracks disease-associated microglial activation and phagocytic state, but the ALS evidence is still thin. A recent ALS CSF study assessed sTREM2 alongside NfL, GFAP, YKL-40, and sAPPs, yet the signal emphasized NfL and YKL-40 rather than sTREM2; by contrast, human CSF detectability and disease-stage relevance are well established in AD. **Evidence tier: Preliminary.** Key citations: Rosén C et al., 2024, *Journal of the Neurological Sciences*, “High levels of neurofilament light and YKL-40 in cerebrospinal fluid are related to poor outcome in ALS”; Ewers M et al., 2022, *Neurobiology of Aging*, “Biological correlates of elevated soluble TREM2 in cerebrospinal fluid.” citeturn22search4turn22search10turn22search2

## Complement, coagulation, vascular inflammation, and matrix turnover

**C3 — complement component 3.** C3 is central to complement-mediated opsonization and microglia-dependent synapse elimination, and ALS tissue studies show activation of the complement cascade including C3. The reason C3 remains only a **preliminary CSF biomarker candidate** is not lack of biology, but lack of strong ALS CSF progression-specific protein studies in the sources reviewed; human CSF detectability is nevertheless well documented in other neurologic diseases including AD, PD, and MS. **Evidence tier: Preliminary.** Key citations: Sta M et al., 2011, *Journal of Neuroinflammation*, “Innate and adaptive immunity in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis: evidence of complement activation”; Aiyaz M et al., 2005, *Disease Markers*, “Complement protein isoforms in CSF as possible biomarkers for neurodegenerative disease.” citeturn29search0turn9search0turn29search6

**CFB — complement factor B.** Factor B represents the alternative complement pathway and is attractive for panel diversity because it complements C3 by capturing amplification rather than only downstream deposition. As with C3, the problem is validation depth rather than biology: human CSF detectability exists in other CNS diseases, but direct ALS CSF progression evidence remains sparse in the reviewed sources. **Evidence tier: Preliminary.** Key citations: Aiyaz M et al., 2005, *Disease Markers*, “Complement protein isoforms in CSF as possible biomarkers for neurodegenerative disease”; mechanistic rationale from alternative-pathway biology and complement amplification literature. citeturn9search0turn29search17

**VEGFA — vascular endothelial growth factor A.** VEGFA is a useful bridge between neurovascular support, excitotoxic resilience, and progression biology. Human ALS CSF work found higher VEGF levels in patients with longer disease duration and **slower** progression, while preclinical and mechanistic studies show direct trophic and anti-excitotoxic effects on motor neurons. **Evidence tier: Strong to moderate.** Key citations: Devos D et al., 2004, *Neurobiology of Disease*, “Effects of vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF) on motor neuron degeneration”; Ilzecka J et al., 2014, *Acta Neurologica Scandinavica*, “VEGF levels in CSF and serum in mild ALS patients”; Bogaert E et al., 2010, *Journal of Neurochemistry*, “VEGF protects spinal motor neurons against chronic excitotoxic degeneration.” citeturn19search0turn20search15turn19search10

**MMP9 — matrix metalloproteinase-9.** MMP9 captures a somewhat different process from complement: extracellular-matrix cleavage, blood–CSF barrier remodeling, and inflammatory proteolysis. Recent work confirms active MMP-9 is measurable in both serum and **CSF** in ALS, while older ALS studies linked dysregulated MMP-9 to disease progression; mechanistically, MMP-9 also intersects with osteopontin-dependent late-phase motor-neuron degeneration. **Evidence tier: Moderate.** Key citations: Niebroj-Dobosz I et al., 2009, *Neuromuscular Disorders*, “Serum matrix metalloproteinase-9 activity is dysregulated with disease progression in ALS”; Fang L et al., 2024, *Neurobiology of Disease* (PMID 41009467), “ALS Patients Exhibit Altered Levels of Total and Active MMP-9 …”; Morisaki Y et al., 2016, *Scientific Reports*, “Selective Expression of Osteopontin in ALS-resistant Motor Neurons is a Critical Determinant of Late Phase Neurodegeneration Mediated by Matrix Metalloproteinase-9.” citeturn25search3turn25search0turn25search2turn25search4

**MBP — myelin basic protein.** MBP is not a canonical ALS marker, but it is worth keeping on the exploratory list because it can report blood–brain barrier leakage and myelin injury, both of which may worsen prognosis in ALS-FTD-spectrum disease. The best available study in the reviewed sources linked CSF MBP and BBB dysfunction to worse prognosis, but the signal was stronger in ALS-FTD than in classical ALS. **Evidence tier: Preliminary.** Key citations: Zhu Y et al., 2021, *Neurological Sciences*, “Blood-brain barrier dysfunction and myelin basic protein in survival of ALS and ALS-FTD.” citeturn26search2

**SELENOP — selenoprotein P.** SELENOP gives you an extracellular oxidative-stress/selenium-transport candidate that is directly measurable in ALS CSF and serum. The available ALS case-control study supports detectability in both compartments; however, the reviewed sources did not provide strong progression-rate-specific CSF evidence, so it remains more useful as a biologically credible exploratory addition than as a leading prognostic marker. **Evidence tier: Moderate to preliminary.** Key citations: Vinceti M et al., 2022, *International Journal of Molecular Sciences* (PMID 36077261), “Selenoprotein P Concentrations in the Cerebrospinal Fluid and Serum of Individuals with Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis, Alzheimer’s Disease, Mild Cognitive Impairment and Controls”; Saito Y et al., 2023, *Journal of Biological Chemistry*, “An efficient selenium transport pathway of selenoprotein P into the brain.” citeturn28search0turn28search1

## Axonal degeneration, synaptic dysfunction, and extracellular matrix remodeling

**NEFL — neurofilament light chain.** NfL is the most mature ALS progression biomarker in CSF and probably the single strongest protein to anchor a progression-rate panel. Multiple human ALS CSF studies show that higher NfL tracks faster functional decline, more aggressive disease trajectories, shorter time to spread, and poorer survival; it is also straightforwardly detectable in human CSF across many neurodegenerative disorders. **Evidence tier: Strong.** Key citations: Poesen K et al., 2017, *Neurology*, “Neurofilament markers for ALS correlate with extent of upper and lower motor neuron disease”; Dreger M et al., 2021, *Frontiers in Neuroscience*, “Cerebrospinal Fluid Neurofilament Light Chain Predicts Disease Aggressiveness in Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis: An Application of the D50 Disease Progression Model”; Gaiottino J et al./Menke RAL et al. progression-related CSF NfL studies; Sun Q et al., 2020, *Frontiers in Neurology*, “CSF Neurofilament Light Chain Elevation Predicts ALS Severity and Progression.” citeturn26search5turn26search6turn39search2turn39search0turn39search9

**NEFH — phosphorylated neurofilament heavy chain, typically assayed as pNfH.** pNfH belongs in the same mechanistic family as NfL but is worth keeping as a separate candidate because some studies suggest complementary prognostic information and greater specificity for motor-neuron disease. Human CSF studies show pNfH is elevated in ALS and correlates with progression rate and prognosis, including discrimination across upper-motor-neuron syndromes. **Evidence tier: Strong.** Key citations: Poesen K et al., 2017, *Neurology*, “Neurofilament markers for ALS correlate with extent of upper and lower motor neuron disease”; Abu-Rumeileh S et al., 2020, *Clinical Chemistry and Laboratory Medicine*, “Diagnostic and prognostic value of CSF neurofilaments in a cohort of patients with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis”; Verde F et al., 2021, *Biomedicines*, “CSF Heavy Neurofilament May Discriminate and Predict Motor Neuron Diseases.” citeturn39search2turn26search5turn26search7

**APP — amyloid precursor protein, represented in CSF chiefly by sAPPα and sAPPβ fragments.** APP processing is relevant here not because ALS is an amyloid disease, but because CSF sAPP fragments appear to index axonal transport and neuroprotective processing capacity. In a dedicated ALS study, lower CSF sAPPα/β and higher NfH/sAPP ratios were associated with **fast progression**, while sAPP assays are analytically well established in human CSF from AD/FTD work. **Evidence tier: Strong.** Key citations: Steinacker P et al., 2011, *PLOS ONE*, “Soluble Beta-Amyloid Precursor Protein Is Related to Disease Progression in Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis”; Willemse EAJ et al., 2016, *Journal of Neurochemistry*, “Validation of soluble amyloid-β precursor protein assays as diagnostic tools in CSF”; early CSF sAPP detectability papers in AD. citeturn38search1turn38search7turn37search10turn17search3turn17search4

**CNTN1 — contactin-1.** CNTN1 is mechanistically persuasive because it stabilizes paranodal axon–myelin junctions and therefore speaks to axonal domain failure rather than generic injury. I did **not** find direct ALS CSF progression studies in the reviewed sources; however, human CSF detectability and prognostic relevance are established in multiple sclerosis, which makes CNTN1 a legitimate **exploratory ECM/axonal-domain candidate** for an untargeted CSF proteomics panel. **Evidence tier: Preliminary.** Key citations: Chatterjee M et al., 2019, *Multiple Sclerosis Journal – Experimental, Translational and Clinical*, “Contactin-1 and contactin-2 in cerebrospinal fluid as potential biomarkers for axonal domain dysfunction in multiple sclerosis”; Wang C et al., 2022, *Nature Communications*, “Structural insights into the contactin 1-neurofascin 155 adhesion complex formation.” citeturn31search0turn31search3turn13search3

**TNR — tenascin-R.** TNR is one of the more compelling ECM candidates because it is CNS-restricted, participates in perineuronal nets, myelination, and axonal stabilization, and is directly measurable in human CSF. The limitation is that ALS-specific CSF progression data were not identified in the reviewed sources; CSF detectability comes mainly from AD ECM studies, so TNR should be treated as a **mechanistically strong but clinically preliminary** candidate. **Evidence tier: Preliminary.** Key citations: Minta K et al., 2019, *Journal of Alzheimer’s Disease*, “Cerebrospinal Fluid Concentrations of Extracellular Matrix Proteins in Alzheimer’s Disease”; Pesheva P, Spiess E, Schachner M., 2013, *Journal of Neurochemistry*, “Tenascin-R: Role in the central nervous system”; Suttkus A et al. and related TNR/PNN biology. citeturn32search7turn32search0turn32search10turn32search12turn32search2

## Proteostasis, lysosomal stress, and secreted homeostatic proteins

**UCHL1 — ubiquitin carboxyl-terminal hydrolase L1.** UCHL1 is not secreted in the classic sense, but it is robustly measurable in CSF and biologically relevant as a neuronal proteostasis and injury marker. In ALS CSF, targeted MRM showed UCHL1 elevation, and the increase was especially linked to **short-lived** patients; CSF detectability is also established in other neurodegenerative conditions. **Evidence tier: Strong to moderate.** Key citations: Zhu S et al., 2019, *Molecular Neurobiology*, “Targeted Multiple Reaction Monitoring Analysis of CSF Identifies UCHL1 and GPNMB as Candidate Biomarkers for ALS”; Stukas S et al./other UCHL1 CSF studies across neurodegeneration. citeturn31search5turn15search11turn15search7

**CTSD — cathepsin D.** CTSD is a lysosomal protease and therefore fits the autophagy–lysosome/protein-clearance axis that is central to ALS biology. In ALS CSF, CTSD was increased in the targeted MRM study, but unlike GPNMB or UCHL1 it was not the clearest survival discriminator; still, its mechanism and detectability make it a reasonable moderate-tier candidate. **Evidence tier: Moderate.** Key citations: Zhu S et al., 2019, *Molecular Neurobiology*, “Targeted Multiple Reaction Monitoring Analysis of CSF Identifies UCHL1 and GPNMB as Candidate Biomarkers for ALS”; Valdez C et al./cathepsin D AD biomarker literature. citeturn31search5turn15search10turn15search14

**CST3 — cystatin C.** Cystatin C is one of the better-supported non-neurofilament ALS CSF candidates. The classic PLOS ONE study showed that longitudinal direction of CSF cystatin C change correlated with ALS progression rate and that baseline CSF levels predicted survival; later studies confirmed CSF detectability in ALS, though they were more diagnostic than prognostic. **Evidence tier: Strong.** Key citations: Wilson ME et al., 2010, *PLOS ONE*, “Cystatin C: A Candidate Biomarker for Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis”; Zhang Y et al., 2015, *International Journal of Clinical and Experimental Pathology*, “Measurement of cystatin C levels in the cerebrospinal fluid of patients with ALS.” citeturn37search4turn6search0turn37search5

**GRN — progranulin.** Progranulin is biologically compelling because it is secreted, anti-inflammatory, and tightly linked to lysosomal function and microglial regulation. In ALS CSF, the APP/sAPP study showed that CSF progranulin **decreased with ongoing disease**, suggesting state dependence, and human CSF detectability is well established in genetic FTD proteomics. **Evidence tier: Moderate.** Key citations: Steinacker P et al., 2011, *PLOS ONE*, “Soluble Beta-Amyloid Precursor Protein Is Related to Disease Progression in Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis”; Meeter LHH et al., 2018, *Neurology*, “Novel CSF biomarkers in genetic frontotemporal dementia identified by proteomics”; Bossù P et al., 2015, *Brain, Behavior, and Immunity*, “Inflammatory molecules in Frontotemporal Dementia.” citeturn38search1turn37search10turn15search13turn15search9

## Ranked literature-merit shortlist

The ranking below is meant for **candidate panel construction before any data-driven pruning**. “Strong” means there is direct human ALS CSF evidence tied to progression rate, survival, or future decline. “Moderate” means the protein is measured in ALS CSF and biologically credible, but progression-specific evidence is weaker. “Preliminary” means the mechanistic case is real and human CSF detectability exists, but direct ALS CSF progression validation is not yet strong.

| Rank band | Proteins | Why they sit here | Evidence |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Strong core** | **NEFL, NEFH, CHIT1, CHI3L1, CHI3L2, GPNMB, GPC1, APP, CST3** | Best combination of human ALS CSF detectability and direct links to progression rate, survival, or poor outcome. These are the most defensible first-pass panel anchors. | citeturn26search5turn26search6turn39search2turn28search7turn36search1turn34view0turn31search5turn38search1turn37search4 |
| **Strong-to-moderate add-ons** | **VEGFA, UCHL1** | Both have ALS CSF evidence tied to prognosis/short survival, but the evidence base is smaller or more assay-specific than for neurofilaments or chitinases. | citeturn20search15turn19search0turn31search5 |
| **Moderate candidates** | **GFAP, CCL2, CTSD, GRN, MMP9, SELENOP** | Good mechanistic plausibility and ALS or cross-disease CSF detectability, but progression-rate discrimination is less consistently shown. | citeturn34view0turn21search4turn31search5turn38search1turn25search0turn28search0 |
| **Preliminary exploratory candidates** | **TREM2, C3, CFB, CNTN1, TNR, MBP** | Useful for mechanistic coverage, especially microglial state, complement, and ECM/axonal-domain remodeling, but the reviewed literature does not yet support them as top-tier human ALS CSF progression markers. | citeturn22search2turn29search0turn9search0turn31search0turn32search7turn26search2 |

If you want a **pragmatic literature-merit panel of about 20 proteins** rather than the full exploratory slate, the most defensible version from this review would be: **NEFL, NEFH, CHIT1, CHI3L1, CHI3L2, GPNMB, GPC1, APP, CST3, VEGFA, UCHL1, GFAP, CCL2, CTSD, GRN, MMP9, SELENOP, TREM2, C3, and CNTN1**. That gives you strong coverage across glial inflammation, axonal injury, proteostasis/lysosomes, vascular–matrix remodeling, and complement. citeturn26search5turn28search7turn34view0turn31search5turn38search1turn37search4turn20search15turn25search0turn29search0turn31search0

## Open questions and limitations

The strongest limitation in the ALS CSF literature is **uneven validation depth across mechanisms**. Chitinases and neurofilaments have multiple cohorts and clear progression links, but many complement/coagulation and ECM proteins are still supported more by mechanism and cross-disease CSF detectability than by dedicated human ALS fast-vs-slow studies. citeturn27search14turn34view0turn29search0turn32search7

A second limitation is that some highly plausible proteins — especially **CNTN1, TNR, C3, CFB, and TREM2** — are currently better justified as **mechanistic coverage variables** than as established ALS prognostic markers. If your eventual modeling goal is a biologically interpretable multiaxial panel rather than a minimal clinical-grade prognostic assay, including a few such exploratory proteins is reasonable; if the goal is a parsimonious, literature-maximal prognostic core, you should lean more heavily on **neurofilaments, chitinases, GPNMB/GPC1, APP fragments, cystatin C, and perhaps VEGFA**. citeturn31search0turn32search7turn29search0turn22search2turn26search5turn36search1turn34view0turn38search1turn37search4turn20search15
